This doesn’t happen too often. Unfortunately, I don’t have precise data on how often exactly and from which countries. However, shouldn’t CS agents know where to find this information instead of asking Verification team? We didn’t know where to find it either until we asked in a relevant channel (#dev-eater-grox-support) because the age restriction is not set by us. This adds unnecessary escalation and, therefore, more friction for customers.

[~accountid:620f8ddea715c600691514af] Thanks for the response, I always welcome constructive challenges!


{quote}UI updates{quote}

While you were mentioned several times that “we have clear indication in our data of which product is weighted“, you might be thinking of PIM, but Eater app (and all it’s services: Provider Menu, Selection, Order, Basket, Invoicing) doesn’t know anything about weighted products. It can only work with unit based SKU’s. The price per kilo you see on the card is not being calculated on the app side, this information is coming from the PIM without notion of any denominators and to the app it’s just a text displayed below the item, we already had cases where the price per kilo wasn’t matching item description / price. Notion regarding final weight is also just a text prepended in the SKU description.

As I mentioned before Eater works with units. Stock communication for example above the item is displayed as {{5 units in stock}}. Screenshot you provided shows plenty of space since it’s a bottom sheet, but our quick add button is also displayed in the product card and basket, where it won’t be able to handle 6+ characters without trimming, perhaps some transformation from 1000 g to 1 kg will be needed. And that’s all assuming this data exists, which is not the case for the Eater App, we have no notion of base denominator (100 / 200 g / ml) and how they relate to the upper nominator (kg / liter), which will create a huge branch in logic for displaying this new information without affecting unit based tracking.

{quote}Price. 

> All these points seem to have been addressed already. we already do everything that’s there with weighted products.{quote}

You can always prove me wrong there. But from the campaigns perspective instead of 1, you now need to have 2 prices, 1 is per unit (100g) and the other one is the one we display. Campaigns only work with unit prices (which will be the main price that is counted), so you won’t be able to have flat discounts and price list work on the KG price (like 1 Euro of the 4.2 EUR per KG). Not to mention that obviously there is no communication where price per kilo is being displayed as the main price and communicated across the app in all places where we have price displayed or could have discount communication.

{quote}Basket and Order teams

> Why? It would be again the same, just fetch the data from the increment and add a multiplier. It should also be the same as it’s being done now{quote}

I wish it was that easy, it is not a trivial engineering task

{quote}So all in all we already have everything in place for weighted items. It’s just a visual change (which has a lot of implications as described initially){quote}

As mentioned above, only PIM is somewhat aware about this, other systems that are owned like Campaigns, GROX, Selections, Eater App little to no idea regarding weighted items.

