Hello Team,
I agree with [~accountid:712020:a7a7d4bc-10ca-44a0-a22e-fa8752330abd] position, and from the legal perspective I would add that:

* Under the law, the authority has the right to request relevant information from all Lithuanian companies, but such a request must be justified (both on factual and legal grounds);
* The request must be official – it should be sent to Bolt’s designated email address, addressed specifically to Bolt, and be signed and stamped (if the authority uses stamps);
* The authority must indicate the specific personal data (e.g. name, surname) that would allow Bolt to properly identify the person and provide additional information;
* If the request meets the above requirements, then Bolt is obliged under applicable law to cooperate with the authority and provide the requested information;
* If it is expected that the authority will submit multiple requests related to Lithuanian language checks, it may be advisable to agree on a process with the authority for how such requests will be submitted – e.g. by setting up a dedicated email address or appointing a contact person – in order to ensure that the process is smooth and properly managed;
* All requests and responses should also be stored in one central place so that, if necessary, it is possible to trace what information was requested and what was provided;
* When providing information to the authority, Bolt should clarify that it does not assume responsibility for the accuracy of the information, as Bolt does not verify the accuracy of data submitted by drivers, etc.

If you have any other questions - please let me know 🙏

