{color:#4c9aff}+*Email from Tarun Lewis Silkin re LD settlement offer: -*+ {color}

* *_Email has been attached and pasted below -_* 

{color:#0747a6}_“Hi Anusha_{color}

{color:#0747a6} _We’ve now had a chance to review this in more detail and, as promised, please find below our initial assessment._{color}

{color:#0747a6} {color}{color:#0747a6}*_LD approach to previous offers_*{color}

{color:#0747a6}  _During the mediation on 17 July 2024, the last offer put forward by Leigh Day was_ {color}{color:#0747a6}+_£249,797,066_+{color}{color:#0747a6}_. There are some gaps in how they reached this figure exactly but at a high level:_{color}

* {color:#0747a6}_this was based on the no. of claimants they had at the time – which was 10,712;_{color}
* {color:#0747a6}_they calculated working time on the basis of 100% of ‘on trip’ time and 75% of online time;_{color}
* {color:#0747a6}_they looked at the schedules of loss for the eight sample claimants and worked out that on average, the NMW shortfall was £12.17 per hour; they proposed to discount this to £10 per hour;_{color}
* {color:#0747a6}_they insisted that it was not appropriate to calculate holiday pay on the basis of the 12.07% uplift. They said that holiday ought to be calculated the ‘pre-April 2024’ way and worked out that on average, claimants were entitled to £3.06 per hour; they proposed to discount this to £2.30 per hour;_{color}
* {color:#0747a6}_they proposed that for claimants that have been online for more than 2 years, they would cap their holiday pay entitlement to just two years;_{color}
* {color:#0747a6}_they added £1,500 per claimant for waiver of future claims; and_{color}
* {color:#0747a6}_£1,000 per claimant for s.1 ERA claims._{color}

{color:#0747a6} _This worked out as, c. £23,319.37 per claimant._{color}

{color:#0747a6} _On 26 July 2024, Tarun spoke with Nigel on the phone. Nigel put forward an offer of_ {color}{color:#0747a6}+_£170,213,362_+{color}{color:#0747a6}_. This was calculated in the same way as above for the most part, but on a “worst-case” basis for LD, meaning:_{color}

* {color:#0747a6}_they calculated working time on the basis of ‘on trip’ time only;_{color}
* {color:#0747a6}_they revised the hourly NMW shortfall to £7.85 (down from £10); and_{color}
* {color:#0747a6}_they revised the holiday pay per hour to £6.16 (which seems like a step backwards reflects the concession of ‘on trip’ time only)._{color}

{color:#0747a6} _This worked out as c. £15,889.97 per claimant._{color}

{color:#0747a6} _We put forward a counter which was refused, and on 16 August 2024, LD responded with a new best and final offer of_ {color}{color:#0747a6}+_£127,930,000_+{color}{color:#0747a6}_. This was calculated on the basis of:_{color}

* {color:#0747a6}_revised claimant figures of 11,630;_{color}
* {color:#0747a6}_taking broadly the same approach as outlined above but applying a discount for accelerated receipt of payment._{color}

{color:#0747a6} _This was based on £11,000 per claimant._{color}

{color:#0747a6} {color}{color:#0747a6}*_LD latest offer_*{color}

{color:#0747a6} _We do not entirely follow the calculations used by Leigh Day to get to the latest settlement offer – the letter sets out the parameters but the “workings” in the attached spreadsheets are very light on detail. If we are open to engaging with the idea of settlement, we can ask LD for more detailed workings of how they have calculated these sums – perhaps for just one of the sample claimants so we can track this through for the others ourselves._{color}

* {color:#0747a6}_LD’s new offer is_ {color}{color:#0747a6}+_£282,757,315.65._+{color}

{color:#0747a6} _This works out as £15,474.05 per claimant._{color}

{color:#0747a6} _Broadly:_{color}

* {color:#0747a6}_LD have adopted a new methodology. They have made various concessions (set out below) and calculated a new settlement offer based on the schedules of loss for the eight sample claimants (as shared with us in March/July 2024)._{color}
* {color:#0747a6}_Once LD have worked out an amount per sample claimant using the new formula, they have added these together and divided by eight, resulting in an average amount of £15,474.05 per sample claimant._{color}
* {color:#0747a6}_They have then multiplied this amount by the entire claimant group, which is now_ {color}{color:#0747a6}+_significantly higher_+{color}{color:#0747a6} _at 18,273, to get to the £282m figure._ {color}
* {color:#0747a6}_LD have made several concessions, these are set out in the letter but at a high level:_{color}
* {color:#0747a6}_despite the ET judgment,  LD are still prepared to put forward an offer based on ‘on trip’ time only;_{color}
* {color:#0747a6}_despite previously saying this wasn’t appropriate, LD have agreed to calculate holiday pay liability using a 12.07% uplift on actual earnings (i.e., they are no longer insisting we use the ‘pre-April 2024’ method of calculating holiday pay using the exact reference period);_{color}
* {color:#0747a6}_LD have applied a discount to expenses (as claimed in the SC’s schedules of loss) of 50% - Note to Bolt, as we never made the KSS offer we have never pointed out the arguments we have on their expenses especially when claimed outside pay reference periods, etc;_{color}
* {color:#0747a6}_LD accept that the eight SCs are not necessarily reflective of the wider claimant group. At a high level, this is because the data we sent them previously shows that among the wider claimant group (or at least, the 8,004 claimants as at the date the data was collated), the average total ‘on trip’ hours per claimant was 1,160. Whereas among the sample claimants, the average total ‘on trip’ hours is much higher at 2,041. In other words, the average claimant’s total ‘on trip’ hours = just 56.81% of the average SC’s total ‘on trip’ hours. On that basis, LD have adjusted the SC’s working hours to just 56.81% of those set out in the schedules of loss for the purposes of this calculation. Note – we don’t quite follow this as a logical basis for their position._{color}
* {color:#0747a6}_Because of the above concession (that the SCs drive more than the average claimant), LD have proposed a_ {color}{color:#0747a6}+_further_+{color}{color:#0747a6} _reduction to expenses of 10% (i.e., after the original 50% reduction). In other words, expenses are discounted overall to just 45% of the original sum claimed._{color}
* {color:#0747a6}_LD are not seeking interest._{color}

{color:#0747a6}_A few further thoughts:_{color}

* {color:#0747a6}_We do not quite follow how LD have calculated the revised ‘total NMW shortfall (with s.17 NMWA uplift)’. We’ve tried working this out by reference to the schedules of loss, but we can’t make sense of how LD have worked this out. I think it would be very helpful to see a copy of how LD have adjusted a sample claimant’s schedule of loss, applying the various concessions outlined above, to arrive at the new figures. But we are mindful that this may be wasted costs given the wider Bolt strategy of delaying liability / any payment – and launching a new model / operating basis.  Anusha – are there any updates on this from your side?_{color}
* {color:#0747a6}_For completeness, given the various concessions that LD have now made, we don’t follow how they  have landed at a figure that is greater on a per-claimant basis (£15,474.05 per claimant) than the offer previously made on 16 August 2024 (£11,000 per claimant). I would have expected this to be < £11,000 per claimant?_{color}
* {color:#0747a6}_LD are (still) claiming an amount per claimant for s.1 ERA 1996 claims. This claim is not pleaded in the master grounds of claim, nor is it referenced in the agreed list of issues. Based on recent case law, we think there is a strong argument that this means the ET is not_ {color}{color:#0747a6}+_required_+{color}{color:#0747a6} _to make an award of compensation for a failure to provide s.1 particulars. The ET may take a different view and distinguish our case on the facts, but for the purposes of settlement at least, we do not agree that it should be taken as a given that the claimants will be entitled to any amount for s.1 claims, as LD seem to think._{color}

{color:#0747a6} {color}{color:#0747a6}*_What we should do:_*{color}

* {color:#0747a6}_Whether we intend to engage in settlement discussions (at all) at this time, it would be worth being able to give the business a fuller update here._ {color}
* {color:#0747a6}_Would the team internally be able to “re-work” our liability modelling (and the last DM / PB offer) for the total number of claimants now – to see how far LD are away from our methodology at the point of settlement.  If we are now adopting the same approach to “time on trip” and “holiday pay” – if expenses are the main point of contention it might be worth pointing that out and addressing this with LD to see if we can get them to revise their position._ {color}

{color:#0747a6} _Best wishes_{color}

{color:#0747a6}_Tarun”_{color}

